<section xml:id="FunctionsandGraphs">
  <title>Functions and Graphs</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      As we saw in the preceding section,
      functions can be represented in many different ways.
      If a rule gives one and only one output for each input,
      it's a function,
      whether we specify that rule using a formula,
      a description, a table, or some other means.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this section we'll discuss another tool for describing,
      and working with, functions.
      You probably already have some experience work working with this tool,
      but in this section we'll develop the idea of a graph in the framework of functions.
      If some of this section is old news to you,
      you'll still want to walk through this carefully,
      to see how graphs and functions are related to each other.
      If, on the other hand, you don't have much experience with them,
      don't panic – we'll build everything from the ground up.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection>
    <title>Functions and Coordinates</title>
    <p>
      We've already discussed the use of tables as a way of describing a function.
      A table is simply an organized list of the inputs for a function together with their outputs.
      An alternative way of doing this is to use coordinate pairs.
      A coordinate pair is a way of writing a given input together with its output,
      written in the form:
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>(\text{ input value } , \text{ output value } )</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      For example,
      suppose we define a function using the following table:
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>5</m></cell>
        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
        <cell><m>9</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      We could also define this function with a list of coordinate pairs for each input and output.
      The table tells us that for the input <m>x=0</m> we get the output <m>f(0)=5</m>.
      As a coordinate pair, we would write this as <m>(0,5)</m>.
      Likewise, we could express the fact that <m>f(1)=4</m> by writing the coordinate pair <m>(1,4)</m>.
      And so on.
      Listing out the remaining inputs and outputs in the same way,
      we could represent this function as a set of coordinate pairs:
      <me>
        \{(0,5),(1,4),(2,9),(3,-2)\}
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      (the curly brackets are used to indicate that this is a set of coordinate pairs).
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite the function given in the table below as a set of coordinate pairs.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell />
            <cell />
            <cell />
            <cell />
            <cell />
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>-4</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Following the pattern of <m>(\text{ input } , \text{ output } )</m> we would write <m>\{(4,0), (7,0), (3,2), (9, -4), (1,6)\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that for a given function <m>h(3)=5</m>. 
          Express this as a coordinate pair.
        </p>
        <p>
          Following the pattern again, 
          we would write this as the coordinate pair <m>(3,5)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          You may notice that coordinate pairs look exactly like interval notation. 
          <m>(3,5)</m> read as a coordinate pair means 
          <q>the input <m>3</m> gives the output <m>5</m></q>; but <m>(3,5)</m> read as an interval means 
          <q>all the real numbers between <m>3</m> and <m>5</m> not including <m>3</m> and <m>5</m> themselves.</q> 
          It is really unfortunate that the same notation can be correctly interpreted as two completely different things. 
          Fortunately, though, 
          this is almost never a problem because it is just about always clear from context whether we are talking about a coordinate pair or an interval.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <p>
      Any function that can be described with a table can be described with coordinate pairs.
      And so, anything we can do with a function as a table we can do with a function as a set of coordinate pairs.
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>\{(0,3), (1,3), (2,5), (3,6), (5,11)\}</m> a function?
        </p>
        <p>
          In this set of coordinate pairs, 
          each distinct input has one and only one output. 
          Therefore this is a function.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>\{(0,5), (1,-3), (3,6), (-2,7), (1,0)\}</m> a function?
        </p>
        <p>
          In this set of coordinate pairs we have both <m>(1,-3)</m> and <m>(1,0)</m>. 
          This means that the input <m>1</m> gets both <m>-3</m> and <m>0</m> as outputs. 
          A function cannot give two different outputs for the same input. 
          This is not a function.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Graphs</title>
    <p>
      Tables, and lists of coordinate pairs, both are very limited.
      If we are working with a function that has just a few possible inputs,
      then tables and lists of coordinate pairs are a simple and efficient way of giving the function.
      But if a function has a big domain with lots of possible inputs,
      producing a table or a list of coordinate pairs is impractical if not impossible.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example,
      let's start by considering a fairly simple function,
      the doubling function <m>f(x)=2x</m>.
      The domain of this function is all real numbers – if we give this function any real number at all,
      the function can double it and give us the result as an output.
    </p>
    <p>
      We could give some <em>sample</em>
      values of this function in a table, such as this:
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell>x</cell>
        <cell>0</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell>3</cell>
        <cell>4</cell>
        <cell>5</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>f(x)</cell>
        <cell>0</cell>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell>4</cell>
        <cell>6</cell>
        <cell>8</cell>
        <cell>10</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      which might be useful to give a sense, by examples,
      of just what this function does.
      But this table does not fully describe the function,
      because it only gives the outputs for the comparatively few selected inputs listed in the table.
      We might assume from this table what the outputs would be for other inputs,
      but we could not, just from this table, know for sure.
      This table certainly looks like we've got the doubling function in mind.
      It certainly looks like if we input <m>6</m> we'd get an output of <m>12</m>.
      But we can't tell for sure.
      This table only tells us the outputs for the specific inputs listed.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the face of it,
      it seems like it would be impossible to create a table that would list all infinitely many inputs and outputs for the doubling function.
      But, it turns out that there actually is a way to do this.
      How do you make a list of all the real numbers?
      Well, in fact you already know a way of doing this:
      by drawing a number line.
    </p>
    <p>
      A number line
      <q>lists</q>
      all the real numbers because every real number corresponds to a point on the line.
      Some of the numbers, such as <m>-3</m>,
      <m>0</m> and <m>5</m> are marked specifically on the number line below.
      But we understand the other numbers are there as well.
      Where is <m>4.75</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      Well, we understand it to be at the point
      <m>\frac{3}{4}</m> of the way between 4 and 5.
      Where is <m>-3,000,000</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      Well, we understand that arrow to the left to mean the line continues forever,
      and so it eventually gets to the right place.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A visualization of all real numbers</caption>
      <image width="73%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure01.png">
        <description>
          A horizontal number line with arrows on both ends indicating it extends infinitely in both directions. The line has tick marks at regular intervals labeled with integers ranging from -10 on the left to 10 on the right, with 0 positioned in the center.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <caption>Descartes: He thought, therefore he was. Source: wikipedia.org</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure02.png">
        <description>
          A classic oil painting portrait of René Descartes.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Descartes' innovation was to draw two number lines,
      one perpendicular to the other,
      to create a plane, called the <em>coordinate plane</em>,
          <idx><h>Coordinate Plane</h></idx>
      or, in his honor, the <em>Cartesian plane</em>
          <idx><h>Cartesian Plane</h></idx>
      <fn>
      \quotes{Cartesian} is derived from the name \quotes{Descartes}. The \quotes{Des} is left off for reasons having to do with the Latin form of his name.
      </fn>.
      The horizontal number line is used to represent all the possible input values of a function.
      It is known as the <em>horizontal axis, or the input axis</em>,
          <idx><h>Coordinate Plane</h><h>Horizontal Axis</h></idx>
      or, since the most common variable name to use for inputs is <m>x</m>,
      the <em><m>x</m>-axis</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The output values for the function are represented using the vertical number line,
      known as the <em>vertical axis, or output axis</em>,
          <idx><h>Coordinate Plane</h><h>Vertical Axis</h></idx>
      or, since the most common variable name to use for outputs is <m>y</m>,
      <em>the <m>y</m>-axis</em>.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A set of coordinate axes.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure03.png">
        <description>
          An image of a blank graph.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Any coordinate pair can be represented as a point in this plane.
      The <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> values of the coordinate pair give its point's location.
      We move right or left along the <m>x</m>-axis to the <m>x</m> value,
      and then from there we move up or down parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis to the <m>y</m> value.
      For example,
      the coordinate pair <m>(3, -2)</m> would be represented by the point in the plane shown below:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>The point <m>(3,-2)</m> marked on a set of axes.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure04.png">
        <description>
          A set of blank coordinate axes with a single point plotted in the fourth quadrant. The point is located three tick marks to the right of the vertical axis and two tick marks below the horizontal axis, corresponding to the coordinates (3, -2).
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Now, we can represent any numerical table as a collection of points in the plane.
      The table we create above,
      giving a few selected inputs and outputs for the function <m>f(x)=2x</m> could be represented by the following points in the plane:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Some input-output pairs for <m>f(x)=2x</m>.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure05.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing several plotted points that represent input-output pairs for the function f(x) = 2x. Starting from the origin (0,0), the points are plotted in the first quadrant, showing a consistent upward and rightward trend: (1,2), (2,4), (3,6), (4,8), and (5,10). Each point is represented by a small square.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Now here's the infinitely clever part.
      If we want to represent all the input-output pairs for a function,
      we can actually do that.
      Notice that the points we plotted for <m>f(x)=2x</m> in the diagram above all seem to fall into a pattern – they seem to fall in a straight line.
      It turns out that if we plotted all the input-output pairs for <m>f(x)=2x</m> they would form a straight line like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>The line formed by input-output pairs for <m>f(x)=2x</m>.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure06.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing a solid straight line passing through the origin (0,0). The line has a positive slope, extending from the bottom-left quadrant (III) through the origin and into the top-right quadrant (I). The line represents the continuous set of all input-output pairs for the function f(x) = 2x.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Note that for the time being,
      you'll have to take it on faith that all the input-output pairs for this function actually do fall into this line;
      in the next chapter we'll see how we know for sure that the input-output pairs are precisely the same as the points on the line.
      It should though seem reasonable to expect there is something more than just a coincidence going on that the points we plotted happen to fit a straight line.
    </p>
    <p>
      For many functions,
      the points for all the input-output pairs will form straight lines like this one.
      There are other possibilities, however.
      For example, if we consider the function
      <m>g(x)=x^2</m> we can create this table:
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell>-3</cell>
        <cell>-2</cell>
        <cell>-1</cell>
        <cell>0</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell>3</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
        <cell />
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
        <cell>4</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>0</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>4</cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      and if we plot the points from this table in the plane we get:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Some of the input-output pairs for the function <m>g(x)=x^2</m>.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure07.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing several plotted points for the function g(x) = x squared. The points are symmetrical across the vertical y-axis, forming a U-shape. Plotted points include (0,0) at the origin, (1,1) and (-1,1), (2,4) and (-2,4), and (3,9) and (-3,9). Each point is represented by a small square.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      which clearly can't be made to fit any straight line.
      It turns out (again,
      we'll see exactly why this is later) that the actual collection of points for this function looks like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>The shape formed by the input-output pairs for <m>g(x)=x^2</m>.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure08.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing a solid parabolic curve for the function g(x) = x squared. The curve is symmetric across the vertical y-axis, with its lowest point, the vertex, at the origin (0,0). The two arms of the parabola open upward, extending into the first and second quadrants.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Even though that's not a straight line,
      it definitely fits a pattern.
      That's huge.
      It turns out that when we plot all the points for all the input-output pairs for just about any function we might care about,
      the result will always be some reasonable shape or pattern.
    </p>
    <p>
      The collection of all the input-output pairs for a function plotted in a coordinate plane is called a <em>graph</em>
          <idx><h>Graph</h></idx>
      of the function.
      We will see in the upcoming chapters of this book that graphs are an incredibly powerful tools for working with functions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before moving on,
      there are a few terms that are commonly used with graphs and the coordinate plane that we should define.
      The point <m>(0,0)</m> that lies in the center where the two axes intersect is called the <em>origin</em>.
          <idx><h>Coordinate Plane</h><h>Origin</h></idx>
      You can think of the origin as the
      <q>starting point</q>
      from which we move to the coordinates of other points.
    </p>
    <p>
      Also, the two axes naturally divide the coordinate plan into four quarters,
      which are called <em>quadrants</em>.
          <idx><h>Coordinate Plane</h><h>Quadrants</h></idx>
      The quadrants are numbered, usually with Roman numerals,
      starting in the upper right and moving around counter-clockwise,
      as shown below:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>The quadrants of the Cartesian Plane.</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure09.png">
        <description>
          An illustration of the Cartesian Plane divided into four quadrants by the x and y axes. Each quadrant is labeled with a Roman numeral in counter-clockwise order: Quadrant I is in the top-right, Quadrant II is in the top-left, Quadrant III is in the bottom-left, and Quadrant IV is in the bottom-right.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Quadrant I is called the <em>first quadrant</em>,
      quadrant II is called the <em>second quadrant</em>, etc.
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the location of the point <m>(-3,1)</m> in the Cartesian plane. 
          In which quadrant is this point located?
        </p>
        <p>
          To locate this point we follow the input (horizontal) axis from the origin to the left to <m>-3</m> and then follow the output (vertical) axis up to a height of <m>+1</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The location of <m>(-3,1)</m> in the Cartesian Plane</caption>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure10.png">
            <description>
              A coordinate plane with a single point plotted in Quadrant II. The point is located three tick marks to the left of the vertical axis and one tick mark above the horizontal axis, representing the coordinates (-3, 1). The Roman numeral II is displayed in the upper-left corner of the plane.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          This point lies in the second quadrant (i.e. Quadrant II).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the location of the point <m>(0,-2)</m> in the Cartesian plane. 
          In which quadrant is this point located?
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the first coordinate is <m>0</m>, 
          we don't move at all to the right or left from the origin. 
          We then follow the output (vertical) axis down to <m>-2</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The location of <m>(0,-2)</m> in the Cartesian Plane</caption>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure11.png">
            <description>
              A coordinate plane with a single point plotted on the vertical y-axis. The point is located two tick marks below the origin, representing the coordinates (0, -2). The Roman numeral III is displayed in the bottom-left corner and the Roman numeral IV is displayed in the bottom-right corner.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          This point does not lie in any quadrant. 
          It is located directly on the output (vertical) axis between Quadrants III and IV.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <p>
      We can create graphs for functions whose formulas we know
      (more on that later).
      We can also, though,
      use graphs even without formulas as a way of describing functions.
      For example,
      suppose you are filling a tub with water at a steady rate.
      The amount of water in the tub is a function of the time
      (since at any given time there is one and only one amount of water in the tub).
      Can we sketch a graph of what this function would look like?
    </p>
    <p>
      If we assume the starting time (when
      <q>time is zero</q>
      ) is when we first started the water flowing in,
      we know that the graph must include the origin,
      since when the time is zero (i.e. when we start) the amount of water in the tub is also zero (i.e. the tub is empty).
      Then as time passes, the amount of water goes up at a steady rate,
      so our graph should also go up at a steady rate until the tub is full,
      at which point the graph would level off.
      So, the graph should look something like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Graph of water in the tub as a function of time</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure12.png">
        <description>
          A line graph where the horizontal axis is labeled "time" and the vertical axis is labeled "water". The graph begins at the origin (0,0) and rises at a constant, positive slope, forming a straight diagonal line. After reaching a certain height, the line becomes perfectly horizontal, extending to the right, indicating that the amount of water remains constant as time continues to pass.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Now, we can't put numbers on this graph because we don't have that information;
      we don't know the rate of the water's flow,
      the volume of the tub, how long it took to fill, etc.
      But we can label the axes with the quantities each axis represents,
      and we can feel confident that this graph communicates the idea of filling the tub at a steady rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice that in this graph we only are looking at the first quadrant.
      This is because our interest lies only with positive values of the variables.
      We are not interested in the amount of water in the tub before we begin to fill it,
      and there could never be negative amount of water in the tub.
      Of course we could include show those empty quadrants if we wanted to for some reason,
      but doing so would serve no purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      When using a graph to describe a function you should always label the graph with as much relevant information as you have available.
      At a minimum, the axes should always be labeled.
      If other information is available,
      that may be included as well.
    </p>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm7">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A radioactive isotope of iodine decays with a half life of 8 hours
          (that is, one half of the isotope decays every 8 hours).
          A lab has a 20 gram sample of this isotope.
          Sketch a graph of the remaining amount of the isotope as a function of time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The amount is decreasing, so our graph should move downward.
          It cannot move down in a straight line, though,
          because if it did the graph would eventually fall below the horizontal axis – meaning a negative amount of iodine,
          which is ridiculous.
          So, the graph must curve downward.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can get a sense of how this would work by plotting a few points based on the information we have.
          Since the amount is reduced by half every 8 hours,
          we can figure out the amounts we have at 8 hour intervals:
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell>Time (hours)</cell>
            <cell>Amount (grams)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell />
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>20</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>16</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>24</cell>
            <cell>2.5</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Roughly plotting these points:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure13.png">
            <description>
              A scatter plot with the horizontal axis labeled "time (hrs)" and the vertical axis labeled "amount (grams)". Four points are plotted, showing a decreasing trend. The first point is on the vertical axis at a high value. The subsequent three points move to the right and downward, with each step down becoming smaller, suggesting an exponential decay or a similar decreasing relationship.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          gives us a sense of what the graph must look like.
          Connecting these dots into a smooth shape and labeling we should end up with a graph something like this:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure14.png">
            <description>
              A coordinate plane with the horizontal axis labeled "time (hrs)" and the vertical axis labeled "amount (grams)". A smooth, solid curve is drawn through the four points from the previous scatter plot. The curve starts high on the vertical axis and slopes downward to the right, leveling off as it approaches the horizontal axis, illustrating a continuous exponential decay over time.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          It is not necessary and probably not even desirable to show all the plotted points specifically labeled;
          but it would be good to label a few values to give a sense of scale.
          A graph such as this would be a good choice:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure15.png">
            <description>
              A coordinate plane with the horizontal axis labeled "time (hrs)" and the vertical axis labeled "amount (grams)". A smooth curve shows exponential decay, starting from a point on the vertical axis marked with the number 20. On the horizontal axis, a specific tick mark is labeled with the number 8, representing a time of 8 hours. The curve continues to decrease as it moves to the right.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <p>
      Graphs can take on all sorts of shapes.
      For example,
      here is graph for the closing price of the Dow Jones Industrial Average (a reflection of the US stock market) as a function of time over the years 2008-2009:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure16.png">
        <description>
          A detailed line graph showing the fluctuations of a value over time, specifically from early 2008 through late 2009. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 14,000 in increments of 2,000. The horizontal axis is labeled with dates such as 1/2/2008, 5/2/2008, 1/2/2009, and 11/2/2009. The blue line starts near 13,000, experiences a sharp and significant drop starting around September 2008, reaching a low point near 6,500 in early 2009, before gradually trending upward through the remainder of the year.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      And of course wilder shapes are possible.
      Is there any restriction on what shapes a graph can take?
      Well, what about a
      <q>graph</q>
      like this one:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Could this be a graph of a function?</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure17.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing a circle centered at the origin (0,0). The circle has a radius of approximately 4 units, passing through the points (4,0), (0,4), (-4,0), and (0,-4). The graph illustrates a relation that fails the vertical line test, as many vertical lines would intersect the circle at two different points.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      This is a nice, smooth, geometrically pretty shape.
      Shapes don't come much nicer than circles.
      But could it be the graph of a function?
      Well, no, actually.
      Remember that a function must give
      <em>one and only one</em>
      output for each input in its domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      The input <m>x=6</m> does not have an output here,
      but that is fine if this were the graph of a function.
      That just means <m>6</m> is not in the domain.
      The problem lies with inputs like <m>x=3</m>.
      What is the output for the input <m>x=3</m>?
      Well based on where the marks on the axis are located,
      it looks as though the point <m>(3,4)</m> is on the graph,
      so we might think the output must be <m>y=4</m>.
      Or, in other words, <m>f(3)=4</m>.
      But the point <m>(3,-4)</m> is also appears to be on the graph,
      so the output for <m>x=3</m> must also be <m>y=-4</m>.
      Or in other words, <m>f(3)=-4</m> too.
      And that is a problem.
      Remember, a function must always give a straight answer!
      For the input <m>x=3</m> the
      <q>graph</q>
      cannot show two different outputs.
      Therefore this circle cannot be the graph of a function.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's nothing special about the input <m>x=3</m>.
      We could have used lots of other choices for <m>x</m> and run into the same problem.
      In fact, we could have used any input where the graph shows to different outputs.
      We don't even need to be able to tell what the specific outputs are.
      If we used the input <m>x=-2.5</m>, say,
      we might not be able to tell very well what the corresponding outputs are,
      but we could tell that there are two of them.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Drawing a vertical line shows how the input value we've drawn it through has two outputs</caption>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure18.png">
        <description>
          A coordinate plane showing a circle centered at the origin. A solid vertical line is drawn through the left side of the circle, intersecting it at two distinct points: one in the second quadrant and one in the third quadrant. This visual demonstrates that for a single input value (x-coordinate), there are two different output values (y-coordinates), confirming that the circle does not represent a function.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The observation we are making here is sometimes referred to as the vertical line test.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
              <idx><h>Vertical Line Test</h></idx> <em><em>The Vertical Line Test</em></em>
        </p>
        <p>
          A graph is the graph of a function if, and only if,
        </p>
        <p>
          you can never draw a vertical line which intersects the graph more than once.
        </p>
        <p>
          OR equivalently
        </p>
        <p>
          A graph is the graph of a function if, and only if,
        </p>
        <p>
          every vertical line you can draw will intersect the graph at most one time.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Because the only requirement of a function is that it give one and only one output for each input,
      the only requirement for a graph to be a function is the vertical line test.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>The Graphing Calculator</title>
    <p>
      Throughout this course, we will be using graphs extensively.
      A graphing calculator is required for this course,
      and before we conclude this section we will introduce the use of the calculator to obtain graphs of functions when we know their formulas.
      (We'll just introduce the basics here,
      and expand on the use of the graphing calculator in future chapters.)
    </p>
    <p>
      Let's return to the function we started this chapter with,
      <m>f(x)=2x</m>.
      We can use the calculator to obtain a graph of this function using the following process.
    </p>
    <p>
          <idx><h>Calculator:Graphing</h></idx>
      <em>Step 1: Enter the function formula into the calculator.</em>
      We first need to tell the calculator the formula for the function we want to graph.
      (We do this the same way that we entered formulas to create tables in Chapter ??.)
      First, hit the
      <q>Y=</q>
      key in the upper left corner of your TI-83 or 84,
      and then enter the formula on the first (
      <q>Y1=</q>
      ) line.
      As with tables, the other lines (
      <q>Y2=</q>,
      <q>Y3=</q>
      etc.) are there to allow you to enter more than one formula at a time – not something we care about right now.
      Also as with tables, the formula must be written with
      <q>X</q>
      as the input variable.
      Use the
      <q>X,T<m>,\Theta</m>,n</q>
      key to get an
      <q>X</q>. The resulting screen should look like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure19.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "Y=" editor screen on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. At the top, "Plot1", "Plot2", and "Plot3" are listed. Below, several function slots are shown, with "Y1=2X" entered in the first slot. The remaining slots, "Y2" through "Y7", are empty. The equal sign next to Y1 is highlighted, indicating that the function is selected to be graphed.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
          <idx><h>Calculator:Window Settings</h></idx> <em>Step 2: Set the window.</em> The Cartesian plane extends out to infinity in all directions, and many graphs do as well.
      So of course there is no way to display the whole plane on the calculator screen.
      We need to tell the calculator what portion of the plane we want it to display.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the calculator's top row there is a key labeled
      <q>WINDOW</q>. Hit that key now,
      and it will take you to a screen that should look like this (though the specific numbers you see may not be the same):
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure20.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "WINDOW" settings screen on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. The settings are: Xmin=-8, Xmax=8, Xscl=1, Ymin=-6, Ymax=6, Yscl=1, and Xres=1. This defines the coordinate range and scale that will be visible on the graph screen.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The
      <q>Xmin</q>
      and
      <q>Xmax</q>
      tell the calculator the interval of <m>x</m> (input) values to show on the graph.
      On the screen shown above as an example,
      the calculator would show the portion of the graph that lies between <m>x=-8</m> and <m>x=8</m>.
      Likewise, the
      <q>Ymin</q>
      and
      <q>Ymax</q>
      determine the range of <m>y</m> (output) values used;
      in this window it would be the portion of the graph that lies between <m>y=-6</m> and <m>y=6</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <q>Xscl</q>
      and
      <q>Yscl</q>
      determine where the marks on the axes occur.
      These both being set to 1 tells the calculator to mark both axes in intervals of 1 unit.
      The
      <q>Xres</q>
      helps determine how precisely the calculator sketches the points on the graph;
      the precise way in which this is used need no concern us right now.
      We will just leave that at the default of Xres=1.
    </p>
    <p>
      The settings to enter here will depend on what portion of the graph you want to look at in any particular situation.
      The default,
      which we'll use whenever we don't have reason to look elsewhere,
      is the standard window,
      which ranges from <m>-10</m> to <m>+10</m> on both axes,
      with hashmarks (ticks) every one unit.
      So, to set things to the standard window,
      we would want the following settings:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure21.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "WINDOW" settings screen on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator, showing the "Standard" viewing window values. The settings are: Xmin=-10, Xmax=10, Xscl=1, Ymin=-10, Ymax=10, Yscl=1, and Xres=1. A black cursor box is positioned over the negative sign of the Xmin value.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3: Get the graph.</em>
      In the upper right corner, you'll see a key labeled
      <q>GRAPH</q>. Hit that key now and you should see this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure22.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the graph of the function Y1=2X. The graph is a straight line passing through the origin (0,0) with a positive slope. The axes are visible with tick marks based on the previously defined window settings, showing the line extending from the bottom-left to the top-right of the display.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
          <idx><h>Calculator:Zoom</h></idx>
      As an alternative, when you want a graph in the standard window, you can just hit the
      <q>ZOOM</q>
      key in your calculator's upper row, and select
      <q>6: ZStandard</q>:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure23.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "ZOOM" menu on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. The menu shows several options: 1:ZBox, 2:Zoom In, 3:Zoom Out, 4:ZDecimal, 5:ZSquare, 6:ZStandard, and 7:ZTrig. The first option, "1:ZBox", is highlighted with a black cursor box.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      This will take you directly to the graph in the standard window,
      essentially combining Step 2 and Step 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>It is important to remember that the calculator only shows the portion of the graph that our window instructions tell it to.</em>
      It should be apparent that the graph of <m>f(x)=2x</m> continues above and below the graph we generated above.
      If you wish to see those portions of the graph,
      you can adjust your window settings and see them.
      But whether we see them or not, they are there,
      and while we can't see them on the calculator's screen with our actual eyes,
      we can see them with the mind's eye.
    </p>
    <p>
      Following these steps,
      we can now create a graph for just about function defined with a formula.
      Our goal is of course not just to admire these graphs' beauty – we want to use them as a tool,
      and as we proceed through the course we'll see more and more how to do this.
      Before leaving this chapter,
      though, we'll mention at least one use for these graphs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since a function's graph consists of all its input-output pairs,
      we can use graphs to find the output for any given input.
      The calculator allows you to
      <q>walk</q>
      along the graph,
      telling you the coordinates of points you encounter along the way.
      In the top row of your calculator you will see a key labeled
      <q>TRACE</q>. If you hit this key, you'll see a
      <q>blob</q>
      placed somewhere on the graph,
      and the input and output values of its coordinates:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure24.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the "Trace" function on the graph of Y1=2X. A cursor is positioned on the line in the first quadrant. At the top-left, the function "Y1=2X" is displayed. At the bottom of the screen, the coordinates for the cursor's current position are shown: X=2.7659574 and Y=5.5319149.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      If you don't see the coordinate values, hit
      <q>2nd</q>
      <q>ZOOM</q>
      (that is,
      <q>FORMAT</q>
      ) and make sure your settings are like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure25.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "FORMAT" menu on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. The menu displays various toggle options for the graph's appearance: RectGC or PolarGC, CoordOn or CoordOff, GridOff or GridOn, AxesOn or AxesOff, LabelOff or LabelOn, and ExprOn or ExprOff. Currently, RectGC, CoordOn, GridOff, AxesOn, LabelOff, and ExprOn are selected, as indicated by the black highlighting.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      You can now move around to different points on the graph by using the right and left directional keys in the upper right portion of your calculator.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you do this you'll notice that not every point on the graph is visited when you trace.
      That can't be avoided – there are infinitely many points on the graph,
      so there's no way you could visit them all!
      To visit the point on the graph associated with a given input value, hit
      <q>2nd</q>
      <q>TRACE</q>
      (that is,
      <q>CALC</q>
      ) in the top row to get this menu:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure26.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of the "CALCULATE" menu on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. The menu lists several computational options: 1:value, 2:zero, 3:minimum, 4:maximum, 5:intersect, 6:dy/dx, and 7:∫f(x)dx. The first option, "1:value", is currently highlighted with a black cursor box.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Select
      <q>1: value</q>
      and you'll be returned to the graph with a screen that looks like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure27.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the graph of Y1=2X with a prompt for input. In the bottom-left corner, "X=" is displayed, indicating the calculator is waiting for the user to enter a specific x-value to calculate the corresponding y-value on the line.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Now you can enter the input (<m>x</m>) value you want to visit.
      If we want, say,
      to visit the point that goes with <m>x=-3</m> we get:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure28.png">
        <description>
          A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the result of the "value" calculation for the function Y1=2X. A cursor is positioned on the line in the third quadrant at X=-3. In the bottom-left, "X=-3" is displayed, and in the bottom-right, the calculated result "Y=-6" is shown, confirming the point (-3, -6) on the graph.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <algorithm xml:id="FunctionsandGraphsAlgorithm8">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Obtain a graph for <m>h(t)=\frac{t^3+3t^2}{12}</m> in the standard window, 
          and use this graph to find <m>h(2)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Following the steps outlined above, 
          we first need to enter the function's formula into the calculator. 
          Recall that the calculator only understands X as the input value, 
          so we need to rewrite that formula replacing the <m>t</m> with an X:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure29.png">
            <description>
              A screenshot of the "Y=" editor screen on a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator. A complex rational function is entered into the Y1 slot. The expression shown is "(X^3+3X^2)/12". The remaining slots, Y2 through Y6, are empty, and the equal sign for Y1 is highlighted, indicating it is selected for graphing.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Now, either directly adjusting the window setting to the standard window and hitting 
          <q>GRAPH</q>, or just using 
          <q>ZOOM</q> 
          <ndash />&gt; 
          <q>6: ZStandard</q>, we get:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure30.png">
            <description>
              A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the graph of the rational function (X^3+3X^2)/12. The curve passes through the origin (0,0) and has a local maximum in the second quadrant. It crosses the x-axis at x=-3 and x=0. To the right of the origin, the graph rises steeply into the first quadrant, while to the left of x=-3, it falls steeply into the third quadrant.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Then using 
          <q>2nd</q> 
          <q>TRACE</q> 
          (that is, 
          <q>CALC</q>
          ) <ndash />&gt; 
          <q>1: value</q> 
          and entering 
          <q>X=2</q> 
          we get:
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Figure31.png">
            <description>
              A screenshot of a TI-84 Plus graphing calculator screen showing the result of the "value" calculation for the function Y1=(X^3+3X^2)/12. A cursor is positioned on the curve in the first quadrant at X=2. In the bottom-left, "X=2" is displayed, and in the bottom-right, the calculated result "Y=1.6666667" is shown, which represents the fraction 5/3.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          So, we conclude that <m>h(2)=1.6666667</m> which we would either recognize as 
          <m>h(2)=\frac{5}{3}</m> or round to something reasonable such as <m>h(2) = 1.67</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <subsubsection>
      <title>Coordinates</title>
      <exercises><exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite the function given in the table below as a set of coordinate pairs.
          </p>
          <tabular>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>x</m></cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>8</cell>
              <cell>12</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>7</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>0</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>\{(1,2), (2,2), (4,7), (8,1), (12,0)\}</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite the function given in the table below as a set of coordinate pairs.
          </p>
          <tabular>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>t</m></cell>
              <cell>0</cell>
              <cell>-3</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>3</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>h(t)</m></cell>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>7</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>11</cell>
              <cell>-1</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>f(2) = -1</m>.
            Express this as a coordinate pair.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(2, -1)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>g(0) = 3</m>.
            Express this as a coordinate pair.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite the function given by the following set of coordinate pairs as a table,
            assume <m>f(x)</m>:
            <m>\{(1,3), (2,5), (3,-1), (5,-2)\}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <me>
            \begin{array}{c|c}
            x \amp  f(x) \\
            \hline
            1 \amp  3 \\
            2 \amp  5 \\
            3 \amp  -1 \\
            5 \amp  -2
            \end{array}
          </me>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite the function given by the following set of coordinate pairs as a table,
            assume <m>g(t)</m>:
            <m>\{(1,5), (2,-1), (4,6), (7,-3)\}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The coordinate pair <m>(5,3)</m> gives an input and output for the function <m>f(x)</m>.
            Does this mean <m>f(3)=5</m> or that <m>f(5) = 3</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>f(5)=3</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The coordinate pair <m>(2, -7)</m> gives an input and output for the function <m>g(t)</m>.
            Does this mean <m>g(2) = -7</m> or <m>g(-7)=2</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(0,2), (-1,3), (1, -3), (2,8), (-2, -8)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Yes, each input has one and only one corresponding output.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(0,5), (2,3), (2,5), (4,6), (4,8)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(1,1), (2,1), (3,1)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Yes, each input has one and only one corresponding output.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(0,0), (0,1), (1,2), (2,3), (3,4)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            No, because the input <m>0</m> has two different outputs
            (<m>0</m> and <m>1</m>).
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>\{(-1,2), (-2,2), (-3,2), (-4,2)\}</m> a function?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises></subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
      <title>Graphs</title>
      <exercises><exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to create a table of values for the function <m>f(x)=(x+1)(x-1)</m> for the inputs
            <m>x = -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3</m> or create the table yourself by hand.
            Then sketch a coordinate plane and plot these points on it.
            Use the points you plotted to sketch what the graph of <m>f(x)</m> appears to be.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <me>
            \begin{array}{c|c}
            x \amp  f(x) \\
            \hline
            -3 \amp  8 \\
            -2 \amp  3 \\
            -1 \amp  0 \\
            0 \amp  -1 \\
            1 \amp  0 \\
            2 \amp  3 \\
            3 \amp  8
            \end{array}
          </me>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer15a.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a set of plotted points forming a U-shape, similar to a parabola but shifted downward. The points are symmetrical across the vertical y-axis. Plotted points include (0,-1) on the y-axis, (1,0) and (-1,0) on the x-axis, (2,3) and (-2,3), and (3,8) and (-3,8). Each point is represented by a small square.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer15b.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a solid parabolic curve drawn through a set of plotted points. The parabola is symmetrical across the vertical y-axis with its vertex at (0, -1). The curve opens upward, passing through the x-intercepts at (1, 0) and (-1, 0), and continues through points (2, 3) and (-2, 3) as it extends into the first and second quadrants.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to create a table of values for the function <m>f(x)=x-3(x-1)</m> for the inputs
            <m>x = -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3</m> or create the table yourself by hand.
            Then sketch a coordinate plane and plot these points on it.
            Use the points you plotted to sketch what the graph of <m>f(x)</m> appears to be.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch the location of each of the following points in the Cartesian plan,
            and state the quadrant in which each is located.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(2,1)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-1,2)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(5,-1)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(0,2)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  First quadrant
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer17a.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane with a single point plotted in Quadrant I. The point is located two tick marks to the right of the vertical axis and one tick mark above the horizontal axis, representing the coordinates (2, 1).
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Second quadrant
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer17b.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane with a single point plotted in Quadrant II. The point is located one tick mark to the left of the vertical axis and two tick marks above the horizontal axis, representing the coordinates (-1, 2).
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Fourth quadrant
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer17c.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane with a single point plotted in Quadrant IV. The point is located four tick marks to the right of the vertical axis and one tick mark below the horizontal axis, representing the coordinates (4, -1).
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  none, between first and second quadrants
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer17d.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane with a single point plotted on the positive vertical axis (y-axis). The point is located two tick marks above the horizontal axis (x-axis), representing the coordinates (0, 2).
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch the location of each of the following points in the Cartesian plan,
            and state the quadrant in which each is located.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-3,1)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(1,-3)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-1,-3)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-2,0)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for each of the functions described.
            Label your axes, and, if available,
            include any other relevant information in your graph as well.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  the remaining balance on a mortgage as a function of time as the loan is being paid
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  the monthly sales of hybrid cars as a function of the price per gallon of gasoline
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  the height of a sunflower as a function of time since the seed was planted
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Possible graphs, answers will vary
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer19a.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane showing a single curve in the first quadrant. The curve starts at a point on the positive vertical axis and curves downward and to the right, eventually meeting the positive horizontal axis. The shape represents the upper-right portion of a circle or a concave-down decreasing function.
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer19b.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane showing a single curve in the first quadrant. The curve starts at a point on the positive vertical axis (y-intercept) and increases as it moves to the right. The curve is concave up, meaning its slope becomes steeper as the x-values increase. The horizontal axis includes several tick marks indicating scale.
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <figure>
                    <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer19c.png">
                      <description>
                        A coordinate plane showing a single curve in the first quadrant, resembling an S-shape or logistic growth curve. The curve begins near the origin, increases slowly at first, then more rapidly, and finally levels off as it approaches a horizontal asymptote at the top of the graph.
                      </description>
                    </image>
                  </figure>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for each of the functions described.
            Label your axes, and, if available,
            include any other relevant information in your graph as well.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  the height of a ball as a function of time since it was thrown upward
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  the risk of a heart attack as a function of a man's weight
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  the amount of oil in a reservoir as a function of time since production from the reservoir began
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each of the given scenarios,
            make a reasonable sketch of what the graph of noise in the college's basketball area as function of time might look like:
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Everyone was excited at the start of the game,
                  since the two teams were bitter rivals in close competition for the league championship.
                  The first half was close,
                  with the two teams pretty well matched and neither team able to get much of a lead.
                  But in the second half,
                  the home team quickly fell behind and never even got close again,
                  finally losing by 18 points.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  No one really expected much from this game,
                  since the visiting team was heavily favored,
                  but the home team hung in there and kept the game close through the first half and the first part of the second half.
                  But then the home team had a terrific run and broke away to a 7 point lead.
                  The visitors came back and made it close,
                  taking the lead again just before the buzzer,
                  but the home team put the game away on a last second 3 pointer.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The visiting team already had the league championship locked up,
                  and for the home team this was the last game of a disappointing season.
                  The visitors quickly built up a sizeable lead,
                  and the game never was even competitive.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            There are many possible answers,
            but all should show the noise level getting higher when the game is more exciting and lower when the game is less exciting
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each of the given scenarios,
            make a reasonable sketch of what the graph of Zarofire Systems' stock price might look like as a function of time:
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  The stock began the year at a price of $2 per share,
                  and stayed pretty much flat for several months.
                  Then the company reported blow out earnings,
                  and the stock price quickly soared to new highs,
                  after which it continued to rise gradually for the next few months,
                  before leveling off through the end of the year and ending the year at $10 per share.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The stock began the year at around $2 per share,
                  and over the first few months of the year slowly and gradually faded downward.
                  About mid–year it started to move upward again for a month or so,
                  approaching but not quite returning to where it started the year,
                  and then leveled off for a few months.
                  Finally, the company unexpectedly filed for bankruptcy,
                  and on that news the stock fell dramatically and ended the year nearly worthless.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The stock began the year at around $2 per share,
                  and gradually wavered between $1.75 and $2.25 for the first half of the year.
                  A bit past midyear,
                  the company received a buyout offer at $5 per share,
                  and the stock immediately shot up to nearly that price,
                  where it remained until the deal was completed at year's end.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which of the following could be the graph of a function?
            Explain your reasoning.
          </p>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>
                a. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question23a.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane showing a periodic wave function, likely a sine or cosine curve. The wave oscillates symmetrically above and below the horizontal x-axis, passing through the origin (0,0).
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                b. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question23b.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane showing two curves that are reflections of each other across the x-axis. Both curves meet at the origin. One curve opens to the right and left in the upper half-plane (resembling a sideways parabola), while the other does the same in the lower half-plane. This relation fails the vertical line test.
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                c. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question23c.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane showing a cubic-style function. The curve enters from the top-left (Quadrant II), crosses the x-axis to form a local minimum in Quadrant III, passes through the origin, reaches a local maximum in Quadrant I, and then falls steeply through the x-axis into Quadrant IV.
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            a. is a function b. is not a function c. is a function
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which of the following could be the graph of a function?
            Explain your reasoning.
          </p>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>
                a. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question24a.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane showing a bell-shaped curve, similar to a normal distribution. The curve is symmetrical across the vertical y-axis, reaching its peak on the y-axis and tapering off toward the horizontal x-axis on both the left and right sides.
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                b. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question24b.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane displaying two different linear segments that meet. One line passes through the origin with a shallow positive slope. In the first quadrant, a second line with a much steeper positive slope branches off from the first line, creating a "V" or "kinked" shape.
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                c. 
                <image width="60%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Question24c.png">
                  <description>
                    A coordinate plane showing two overlapping curves. One is a semicircle centered at the origin, sitting entirely above the x-axis. The second is an upward-opening parabola with its vertex at the origin. The parabola resides inside the semicircle near the origin but eventually intersects and extends beyond it.
                  </description>
                </image>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises></subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
      <title>Graphing Calculator</title>
      <exercises><exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for
            <m>f(x)=5-x</m> in the standard window.
            Use your graph to find <m>f(2.4)</m>,
            <m>f(-1.3)</m>, and <m>f(-7.2)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>f(2.4)=2.6</m>, <m>f(-1.3)=6.3</m>, and <m>f(-7.2)=12.2</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for the function
            <m>g(x)=7-x/2</m> in the standard window.
            Use your graph to find <m>g(-3.2)</m>,
            <m>g(1.8)</m> and <m>g(-9.8)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to create a graph for the function whose graph you sketched in Exercise <m>15</m>.
            Does the calculator's graph agree with your sketch?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The graph should match.
            (The scale may be different.)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to create a graph for the function whose graph you sketched in Exercise <m>16</m>.
            Does the calculator's graph agree with your sketch?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for the function
            <m>h(t)=-16t^2+64t+5</m> in the window <m>0 \leq t \leq 4, 0 \leq h(t) \leq 160</m>.
            Use your graph to find <m>h(1.75)</m> and <m>h(3.8)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>h(1.75)=68</m> and <m>h(3.8)=17.16</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for the function
            <m>j(s)=-4.9s^2+19.6s+2</m> in the window <m>0 \leq s \leq 4, 0 \leq j(s) \leq 75</m>.
            Use your graph to find <m>j(1.75)</m> and <m>j(3.8)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises></subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
      <title>Grab Bag</title>
      <exercises><exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If possible,
            give a value for <m>q</m> that would make the following set of coordinate pairs a function:
            <m>\{(0,13), (1,9), (2,14), (q, 9)\}</m>.
            If it is not possible to do this, explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            You can use any value except <m>q=0</m> or <m>q=2</m>.
            If you used <m>q=1</m>, then you would have a redundant pair <m>(1,9)</m>,
            but it would still be a function.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If possible,
            give a value for <m>q</m> that would make the following set of coordinate pairs not a function:
            <m>\{(0,13), (1,9), (2,14), (q, 9)\}</m>.
            If it is not possible to do this, explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If possible,
            give a value for <m>x</m> that would make the following set of coordinate pairs a function:
            <m>\{(0,1), (1,5), (x, 1)\}</m>.
            If it is not possible to do this, explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            You can use any value except <m>x=1</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If possible,
            give a value for <m>x</m> that would make the following set of coordinate pairs not a function:
            <m>\{(0,1), (1,5), (x, 1)\}</m>.
            If it is not possible to do this, explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for <m>s(t)</m>,
            the speed of your car as a function of time since you got on the thruway.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            There are several possibilities,
            but any such graph should show you speeding up when getting on the thruway.
            Here is one possibility:
          </p>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer35.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a single curve in the first quadrant that represents a sigmoid or logistic function. The curve starts near the origin, rises with an increasing slope that eventually reaches an inflection point, and then becomes concave down as it approaches a horizontal asymptote at the top of the frame.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for
            <m>f(x)=10-\frac{x^2}{10}</m> in the standard window.
            Use your graph to find <m>f(3)</m>,
            <m>f(-5)</m>, and <m>f(-8.2)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for the temperature inside an oven as a function of time since the oven was turned on.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            There are several possibilities,
            but any such graph should show the oven starting at room temperature and heating up to the set temperature and pretty much staying at that temperature.
            Here is one possible graph:
          </p>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer37.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a curve in the first quadrant that represents an exponential or logarithmic growth function. The curve starts at a point on the vertical y-axis and rises steeply at first. As the x-values increase, the rate of growth slows down, and the curve becomes concave down, approaching a horizontal asymptote at the top of the graph. The horizontal axis is marked with several tick marks.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for the temperature inside a hot oven as a function of time since the oven was turned off.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for
            <m>g(t)=t^3</m> in the window <m>-3 \leq t \leq 3, -50 \leq g(t) \leq 50</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The graph
          </p>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer39.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a cubic-style curve passing through the origin. The curve is symmetrical about the origin (odd symmetry). It enters from the third quadrant, curves upward with a decreasing slope as it approaches the origin, passes through (0,0), and then continues into the first quadrant with an increasing slope. The graph resembles the function y = x cubed.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph for the volume of water in a backyard pool as a function of the depth of the water in the pool.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for in the standard window for <m>h(t)=\frac{10-3t}{2}</m>.
            Use your graph to find <m>h(8.2)</m>,
            <m>h(0)</m>, and <m>h(-9.6)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The graph
          </p>
          <figure>
            <image width="92.5%" source="FunctionsandGraphsImages/Answer41.png">
              <description>
                A coordinate plane showing a single straight line with a negative slope. The line passes through the positive vertical axis (y-axis) at approximately 3 units above the origin and crosses the positive horizontal axis (x-axis) at approximately 4 units to the right of the origin. The line extends from the top-left to the bottom-right of the graph.
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <p>
            <m>h(8.2)=-7.3</m>, <m>h(0)=5</m>, and <m>h(-9.6)=19.4</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your calculator to obtain a graph for
            <m>p(w)=w-2^w</m> in the window <m>-2 \leq w \leq 4, -20 \leq p(w) \leq 5</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In January, the temperature in Frostbite Falls never went above <m>0 \deg C</m>.
            Let <m>f(t)</m> be the temperature there as a function of time since the start of the year.
            If you were to sketch the graph of <m>f(t)</m> for the month of January,
            in which quadrant would the graph be located?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Quadrant IV
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            An agricultural scientist is developing a new fertilizer for corn.
            She will be conducting a study where different amounts of this fertilizer are applied to several hundred test plots.
            The expected yield
            (in bushels)
            of corn from a test plot is a function of the amount of fertilizer applied
            (in pounds).
            The scientist's expected results are given in the table below:
          </p>
          <tabular>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Fertilizer (pounds)</cell>
              <cell>0</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>4</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Expected Yield (bushels)</cell>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>8</cell>
              <cell>9</cell>
              <cell>8</cell>
              <cell>5</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tabular>
          <p>
            Let <m>x</m> be the amount of fertilizer applied,
            and let <m>f(x)</m> be the expected yield.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Sketch a graph of this function by plotting the points for the data given in the table and making a reasonable guess about the shape of the graph based on these points.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  This table gives expected yield as a function of fertilizer applied.
                  Would the actual yield of corn be a function of fertilizer applied?
                  Why or why not?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>p(t)</m> is the population of New York City as a function of time since the start of this year.
            Would the graph of <m>p(t)</m> pass through the origin?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            No it would not.
            The origin is <m>(0,0)</m>,
            so if the graph pass through this point then <m>p(0)=0</m> meaning the population of NYC was 0 at the beginning of the year.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>d(t)</m> is the distance I've travelled on a road trip as a function of time since I left my house.
            Would the graph of <m>d(t)</m> pass through the origin?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which of the following shapes could be the graph of a function?
            Explain your reasoning.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  a circle
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  a horizontal line
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  a triangle
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  a semicircle
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  a vertical line
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            a horizontal line and a semicircle that is either the top half or the bottom half of a circle (any other semicircle would fail to be a function)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which of the following upper-case letters could be the shape of the graph of a function?
            Explain your reasoning.
            <ol marker="a">
              <li>
                <p>
                  V
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  W
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  E
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  O
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  C
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>f(5)=-2</m>.
            Express this as a coordinate pair.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(5,-2</m>)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The point <m>(-3,8)</m> is on the graph of <m>g(t)</m>.
            Does this mean <m>g(8)=-3</m>?
            Why or why not.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The point <m>(2,7)</m> is on the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
            Does this mean <m>f(2)=7</m>?
            Why or why not.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Yes.
            The first value is the input and the second value is the output.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(2)=3</m>, <m>f(3)=5</m>, and <m>f(5)=2</m>.
            Express this as a set of coordinate pairs.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises></subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>
